#%PAM-1.0
# gtklock — screen locker PAM stack (DESIGN §2, SETUP §6).
#
# This is the ONE place fingerprint auth is wired in. The global authselect
# `with-fingerprint` feature is deliberately not enabled (see
# files/scripts/authselect-fingerprint.sh): putting pam_fprintd in system-auth
# short-circuits pam_unix for *login* too, so no password token is captured and
# pam_gnome_keyring cannot unlock the login keyring — which is what forced the
# blank-password keyring this setup used to carry.
#
# Scoping it here gets the convenience without that cost. The screen unlock is
# the high-frequency case (dozens of times a day); the login is once per boot.
#
# `sufficient` means: if the finger is recognised, auth succeeds immediately. If
# fprintd fails, times out, is not enrolled, or is not running, the stack simply
# falls through to `include login` -> system-auth -> pam_unix and gtklock prompts
# for the password as normal.
#
# DELIBERATELY MINIMAL: upstream ships exactly one line, `auth include login`,
# with no account/password/session sections — gtklock only ever calls
# pam_authenticate. Adding those sections would drag pam_selinux/pam_loginuid/
# pam_namespace into a locker that has no business running them, so this file is
# the upstream content plus one line and nothing else.
#
# FAILURE MODE IS BENIGN BY DESIGN. The worst outcome from anything wrong here is
# "type your password to unlock the screen" — it is not on the login path, so it
# cannot lock you out of the machine. That is exactly why the fingerprint lives
# here rather than in system-auth or /etc/pam.d/greetd.
auth       sufficient   pam_fprintd.so
auth       include      login
